  redFlags: [
    "**A cluster of acute watery diarrhoea in a relief camp** - three or more cases in one camp sector within 24 hours, any adult with severe dehydration, or a single death from diarrhoea: treat as cholera until proved otherwise, send a stool sample, start an ORS corner and report to the district surveillance unit the same day. A parallel cluster of fever with jaundice suggests hepatitis E, which carries a high case fatality in pregnancy.",
    "**A single case of fever with maculopapular rash in a crowded camp** - measles in a displaced, undernourished, unvaccinated population spreads explosively and kills; isolate the case, give vitamin A, and start camp-wide vaccination of children 6 months to 15 years without waiting for laboratory confirmation.",
    "**Evidence of water source contamination** - free residual chlorine below 0.5 mg/L at the delivery point, turbid or foul-smelling supply, a submerged or damaged hand pump, a leaking sewer or latrine within 30 metres of a source, or a positive H2S/coliform test: stop the source, chlorinate or supersede it with tankered water, and distribute halogen tablets the same day.",
    "**An unexplained rise in deaths** - a crude mortality rate above 1 per 10,000 per day, an under-5 mortality above 2 per 10,000 per day, a doubling of the pre-disaster baseline, or any cluster of deaths without an obvious cause: this is the single most sensitive indicator that the relief operation is failing and demands immediate investigation and escalation to the district.",
    "**Camp crowding beyond the Sphere minimum** - less than 3.5 square metres of covered space or 15 litres of water per person per day, more than 20 persons per latrine, or more than 250 persons per tap: these breaches predict measles, acute respiratory infection, meningococcal disease and diarrhoea before the cases appear, and are the earliest actionable warning available.",
    "**Stock-out or cold chain failure** - ORS, IV fluids, antibiotics, ASV or essential chronic-disease drugs exhausted; measles vaccine, insulin or the ILR compromised by a power or fuel failure; or anti-TB and antiretroviral treatment interrupted for more than a few days in a displaced population, which risks default, resistance and a wave of late deaths.",
    "**Breakdown of communication or command** - no situation report reaching the district control room for a reporting cycle, mobile networks down with no radio or runner alternative, a camp with no named medical officer, or duplicated and uncoordinated relief with some areas unvisited: the classic sign that the response has lost coordination, and the reason a communication plan with redundancy is written before the season.",
  ],
  pearls: [
    "**Risk = Hazard x Vulnerability / Capacity.** You cannot stop the cyclone, so every disaster risk reduction measure works by reducing vulnerability or by raising capacity. Open or close any answer on this topic with that sentence.",
    "The definition mark is in the last clause: a disaster is an event **beyond the coping capacity of the affected community**, requiring external assistance. An emergency is handled with existing resources; a mass casualty incident overwhelms a facility; a disaster overwhelms a community and damages the responders too.",
    "**Dead bodies from natural disasters do not cause epidemics.** Victims die of trauma, drowning or burns, not of epidemic disease. The only real risk is to those handling bodies (hepatitis B and C, HIV, tuberculosis, gut organisms), managed with gloves, boots and hand hygiene. Mass burial, mass cremation and spreading lime are unjustified; identify and individually bury or cremate with records, because the family needs a death certificate for inheritance, insurance and remarriage.",
    "**It is the survivors, not the dead, who transmit disease.** Epidemics after disasters follow displacement, crowding, broken water supply and interrupted immunisation - so the money goes to water, sanitation, shelter space and measles vaccine, not to disinfecting corpses.",
    "**Measles vaccine plus vitamin A for children 6 months to 15 years is the immunisation priority in a camp**, given regardless of previous status. Mass typhoid or cholera vaccination is not the first answer; safe water and sanitation are. The nuance worth adding is that WHO now recommends **oral cholera vaccine from the global stockpile as an adjunct** in defined high-risk humanitarian settings.",
    "**Triage reverses ordinary clinical priority.** In the clinic the sickest is treated first; in a mass casualty the aim is the greatest good for the greatest number, so the expectant are deferred and the salvageable treated first. Triage is dynamic and repeated at every echelon, and **over-triage of more than about 50% degrades outcome** by swamping the receiving hospital.",
    "**START in one line: 30-2-Can do.** Walking = green; respiratory rate over 30, capillary refill over 2 seconds (or no radial pulse), or cannot obey commands = red; apnoeic after opening the airway = black; the rest = yellow. Only two interventions are allowed - open the airway and stop the bleeding.",
    "**Most lives are saved in the first hour by neighbours, not by outside teams.** That single fact is the justification for community-based preparedness, Aapda Mitra volunteers, school drills and the trained ASHA, and it is the strongest opening line for any 'how will you prepare the community' question.",
    "**Single-session psychological debriefing is not recommended** and may worsen outcome; the correct response is **psychological first aid - Look, Listen, Link** - within the IASC MHPSS pyramid, with specialist care reserved for the minority with persistent disorder.",
    "**Write the acronyms out in full.** NDRF the *Force* (sections 44-45) is not NDRF the *Fund* (section 46), and SDRF means both the State Disaster Response Force and the State Disaster Response Fund. Similarly, remember that the DM Act 2005 contains no provision for declaring a 'national disaster' - L3 means national assistance and coordination, not a legal declaration.",
  ],
