            "**Definition:** mitigation comprises all measures taken **before** a disaster to eliminate or reduce its impact on society and the environment - it accepts that the hazard will occur and acts on vulnerability, whereas **prevention** attempts to remove the hazard or exposure altogether (relocating a settlement out of the flood plain).",
            "**Structural mitigation** alters the physical environment: earthquake-resistant construction and retrofitting of hospitals, schools and lifeline buildings; embankments, drainage and flood-control reservoirs; cyclone shelters on raised plinths; mangrove and shelter-belt plantation; slope stabilisation and check dams; raising the plinth of critical facilities above the historical flood line.",
            "**Non-structural mitigation** alters law, behaviour and knowledge: land-use zoning and enforcement of the National Building Code and seismic zonation, hazard and risk mapping, environmental impact assessment, insurance and risk transfer, early warning systems, legislation such as the Disaster Management Act 2005, school curricula and public education, and above all the **mainstreaming of disaster risk reduction into every development programme**.",
