            "Establish a **casualty collection point** at a safe, accessible, upwind and uphill location with clearly marked red, yellow, green and black areas, adequate light and a controlled entry and exit for vehicles.",
            "Perform **triage** on the principle of the **greatest good for the greatest number**, which reverses ordinary clinical priority: **Red / Priority I** for salvageable life-threatening injury, **Yellow / Priority II** for serious injury that can wait two to four hours, **Green / Priority III** for the walking wounded, **Black / Priority IV** for the dead and the expectant.",
            "Use **START** for speed - 'walking wounded to the green area; respiratory rate over 30, capillary refill over 2 seconds or no radial pulse, or unable to obey commands, is red; not breathing after opening the airway is black; the remainder are yellow' - under sixty seconds per casualty, with only two interventions permitted, opening the airway and controlling gross haemorrhage. Use **JumpSTART** for children.",
            "Tag every casualty with a colour-coded, pre-numbered triage tag that travels with the patient, and **re-triage** at every echelon, because categories change with time and with treatment. Guard against **over-triage**, which swamps the receiving hospital and worsens overall survival.",
