            "Manage the injuries typical of a cyclone: near-drowning and aspiration, blunt and penetrating trauma from flying debris, crush injuries from collapsed buildings (watch for **crush syndrome** - hyperkalaemia, rhabdomyolysis and acute kidney injury - and start fluids before extrication where possible), lacerations and degloving, fractures, electrocution, snake bite and hypothermia.",
            "**Wound management**: thorough wound toilet and debridement, **delayed primary closure** of contaminated wounds, tetanus prophylaxis according to wound category and immunisation history, and antibiotics only where indicated - not as blanket prophylaxis.",
            "**Distribute rather than concentrate casualties**: send patients to several hospitals according to capability rather than all to the nearest, brief the receiving hospital by phone or radio, escort the unstable, and keep a **numbered mass casualty register** with tag number, name, age, sex, address, injuries, triage category, treatment given and disposal - this record is the basis of family tracing, compensation and the later audit.",
