            "**Sanitation and waste**: temporary trench or pit latrines at no more than **20 persons per latrine**, sited more than 30 metres from and downhill of water sources, segregated by sex and lit; hand-washing points with soap; safe disposal of solid waste, animal carcasses and health-care waste; and control of open defecation, which is the commonest route back to cholera.",
            "**Shelter and food**: minimum **3.5 square metres of covered space per person**, protection from rain and cold, about **2,100 kcal per person per day** with attention to infants, young children, pregnant and lactating women, supervised community kitchens with food safety and hygiene of food handlers, and support for breastfeeding with strict control of donated infant formula.",
            "**Vector and vermin control**: source reduction and drainage of stagnant water, larviciding, indoor residual spraying, insecticide-treated nets, and rodent control - remembering that malaria and dengue typically rise six to eight weeks after the flooding, once breeding sites re-establish.",
