            "**Uses:** the situation-analysis step of the planning cycle; District Health Action Plan and facility annual plan preparation; readiness assessment before NQAS, Kayakalp or LaQshya certification; appraisal of a new service such as telemedicine at a CHC or a dialysis unit; district review of a national programme such as the TB elimination programme; team building and problem solving in a group practice.",
            "**Advantages:** quick, cheap, needs no software or statistician, uses the knowledge of the people who will implement the plan and therefore builds ownership, and produces a picture on a single sheet that a mixed group can argue over.",
            "**Limitations:** subjective and dominated by the loudest voice unless facilitated; produces long unprioritised lists; assigns no weight or score, so a trivial strength sits beside a fatal weakness; the internal-external boundary is often disputed; it is a snapshot that dates quickly; and it describes rather than explains, so it must be followed by root-cause analysis (fishbone or Ishikawa diagram, the five whys, Pareto analysis) and by a written action plan.",
            "**Related tools worth naming:** PEST or PESTLE analysis for the external environment alone (political, economic, social, technological, legal, environmental); force field analysis for driving and restraining forces; the TOWS matrix for the strategy conversion; and the PDCA or PDSA cycle for carrying the resulting actions through.",
