      answer: 1,
      difficulty: "moderate",
      explanation:
        "Cost-effectiveness analysis costs the alternatives in money but measures their effects in natural units - here neonatal deaths averted - and compares two different ways of achieving the same objective, giving a cost per death averted or an incremental cost-effectiveness ratio. Cost-benefit analysis would require the deaths averted to be converted into money, which answers a different question (is the programme worth doing at all) and raises the problem of valuing life. Cost-minimisation applies only when the outcomes of the alternatives are known to be identical, which is not the case here. Input-output analysis relates resources consumed to services produced but does not compare alternatives, and work sampling is a work-study method for fixing staffing norms. If the district also wanted to compare this with a cataract programme it would need cost-utility analysis using DALYs averted or QALYs gained.",
