            "**Situation analysis and needs assessment:** review service statistics, survey data (NFHS, IDSP, district data) and disease burden; carry out formative research - KAP survey, focus group discussions, in-depth interviews, observation - to learn what people already believe and do, why they do it, whom they trust and which media they use; map existing resources and channels.",
            "**Define the problem and the specific behaviour to change:** move from a vague problem to a named behaviour in a named group, and identify the barriers to it (knowledge, attitude, skill, access, cost, permission, service quality).",
            "**Set communication objectives:** SMART - specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound - stating the change in knowledge, attitude, practice or service uptake expected, in what proportion of the audience, by when. Communication objectives must be achievable by communication alone.",
            "**Segment and define the audience:** primary (the person whose behaviour must change), secondary (husband, mother-in-law, ASHA, chemist, private practitioner, peers) and tertiary (panchayat, religious and community leaders, teachers, media, policy makers), segmented further by age, sex, literacy, language, residence, occupation, media habits and readiness to change.",
            "**Design the message and choose the appeal:** one central idea, local language and idiom, a clear feasible call to action, a benefit that matters now; select the appeal - rational, emotional, fear with an offered solution, humour, positive deviance, testimonial - and choose the messenger and the branding.",
