            "**Against commercial marketing:** the objective is individual and social welfare, not profit; the product is often an intangible idea or practice; the target is frequently the poorest and hardest to reach rather than the most profitable segment; the benefit is often delayed and invisible while the cost is immediate; the competition is a pleasurable existing behaviour rather than a rival brand; the programme is publicly funded and publicly scrutinised; and success is measured in behaviour change and health outcome rather than sales.",
            "**Against health education:** health education informs and teaches, assuming that knowledge produces action; social marketing researches the consumer first, segments the audience, engineers an attractive exchange, manipulates price and place as well as promotion, and monitors continuously so the offer can be adapted. Social marketing = health education + the marketing mix + consumer orientation.",
            "**The exchange concept:** the audience must gain something they value - health, status, savings, convenience, approval, modernity - that outweighs what they give up in money, time, effort, pleasure or social risk. A message that offers only a statistical reduction in risk thirty years hence is a poor exchange.",
            "**Competition:** the tobacco company's advertising, the convenience of the open field, the pleasure of unprotected sex, the free advice of the untrained practitioner, and the family's existing habit are the competition, and each must be named and countered.",
