            "**Product:** the tangible good (condom, ORS sachet, iodised salt, LLIN, sanitary napkin, contraceptive pill, chlorine tablet), the service (institutional delivery, DOTS, sterilisation, immunisation) or the intangible practice or idea (exclusive breastfeeding, toilet use, quitting tobacco, hand washing), presented as a branded, attractive, well-packaged offer with an obvious benefit and clear instructions for use.",
            "**Price:** the monetary price, deliberately subsidised but usually **not zero**, because a nominal price signals value, reduces wastage and gives the retailer a margin to stock and push it; and the **non-monetary price** - time, travel, waiting, pain, embarrassment, lost wages, lost pleasure and social disapproval - which is often the larger barrier and the more rewarding one to attack.",
            "**Place:** the distribution channel and its convenience - kirana shops, paan shops, chemists, barbers, bus stands and cinemas for condoms; the ASHA as village depot holder; the anganwadi centre; a toilet inside the compound; a delivery point functioning at night with referral transport. Demand without accessible supply destroys credibility.",
            "**Promotion:** advertising, branding, jingles, hoardings, mass and folk media, celebrity endorsement, point-of-sale display, public relations and interpersonal persuasion - the visible part, and the part on which programmes wrongly spend most of their attention.",
            "**Added Ps:** **People** (staff, retailers and frontline workers - selection, training, incentive and supervision), **Partnership** (other departments, NGOs, professional bodies, industry, media), **Policy** (law and regulation that sustain the change - COTPA, the salt iodisation notification), and **Purse strings** (funding source, size and duration).",
