            "**Advantages:** reaches large populations through existing commercial channels at low public cost; uses the private sector's efficiency and reach; the nominal price makes the product valued and the programme partly self-sustaining; it is consumer-oriented and therefore acceptable; it is measurable through sales data; and it preserves choice.",
            "**Limitations and criticisms:** it works best for a tangible, repeatable, low-cost product and much less well for complex, one-off or socially embedded behaviours; even a subsidised price excludes the poorest, so equity must be checked; it can be captured by commercial interests and become brand promotion; it addresses individual behaviour and may distract from the structural and social determinants (poverty, water supply, gender), thereby individualising blame; it is accused of manipulating people and of raising ethical questions about persuasion with public money; it needs a functioning distribution system and continuous funding; and its effects fade when promotion and supply stop.",
