    "The single sentence that opens the answer: climate change does not usually create new diseases; it acts as a **risk multiplier** on existing environmental and social determinants of health, so its effects appear as more of the diseases India already has, in more places, in more seasons, in the people who can least withstand them.",
    "Learn the **three pathways** - direct, ecosystem-mediated, socially mediated - as a spine, and draw them as a flow chart from driver to exposure to health outcome. Almost every mark in a 10-mark question can be hung on this framework.",
    "**Environmental determinants** are air, water, food, shelter, vector ecology and land; **social determinants** are income, occupation, housing, education, gender, social protection and access to care. The examiner wants both lists named as lists, not merged into prose.",
    "**Vulnerability = sensitivity + limited adaptive capacity**, and risk = hazard x exposure x vulnerability. Quoting the IPCC formulation lets you explain why the same heat wave kills a slum-dwelling widow and not the person in the next street.",
    "**Heat exhaustion versus heat stroke** turns on one thing: the **sensorium**. Any altered mental state with a core temperature above 40 degrees C is heat stroke. Cool first, investigate later; antipyretics and dantrolene have no role.",
    "IMD's heat wave definition: maximum temperature of at least **40 degrees C in the plains, 37 degrees C on the coast and 30 degrees C in the hills**, with a departure from normal of 4.5-6.4 degrees C (severe if more than 6.4), or an actual maximum of 45 degrees C (severe at 47 degrees C) whatever the departure.",
    "**Ahmedabad, 2013** is the answer to any question about heat action plans - South Asia's first, built after the 2010 heat wave, on four pillars: public awareness, an early warning and inter-agency coordination system, capacity building of health staff, and reduction of heat exposure. NDMA's 2016 guidelines generalised it nationally.",
    "**NAPCC (2008) has eight missions and health is not one of them.** The health response came separately in 2019 as **NPCCHH** under the National Health Mission, run through NCDC, whose visible outputs are heat and air-pollution sentinel surveillance and state and district health adaptation plans. Naming both, and the gap between them, marks out a prepared candidate.",
    "**Co-benefits** is the argument that wins marks and changes practice: active transport, clean household and ambient air, plant-rich diets and urban green space each improve health here and now, whatever happens to global temperature. Every one of them is also mitigation.",
    "Keep the **numbers you can defend**: about 1.1 degrees C observed warming (IPCC AR6); about 0.7 degrees C over India, 1901-2018 (IMD); India's NDC of 45% emissions-intensity reduction and 50% non-fossil capacity by 2030 with net zero by 2070; national PM2.5 standards of 40 microgram/m3 annual and 60 microgram/m3 for 24 hours against WHO's 5 and 15; and say explicitly when a figure such as WHO's 250,000 excess deaths a year in 2030-2050 is a **projection**.",
