      difficulty: "easy",
      explanation:
        "Buffering creates a zone of a specified distance around a point, line or polygon, and the population falling inside it is then obtained by overlaying the population layer - exactly the catchment question asked. Interpolation estimates values at unmeasured locations to build a continuous surface (fluoride from sampled wells, for instance) and does not answer a distance question. Geocoding converts an address or place name into co-ordinates, and georeferencing ties a scanned map or image to real-world co-ordinates - both are data preparation steps, not analyses. Kernel density estimation smooths scattered case points into a hotspot surface and says nothing about facility catchments. Note that a straight-line buffer overstates access in hilly or riverine terrain, where network (travel-time) analysis is the better tool.",
